problems, such as the greenhouse effect and melting of glaciers.[2-6] As fossil fuels are not easily replaced in the foreseeable future, the capture and conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) are promising technologies for reducing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emissions. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is also a promising C1 source in synthetic chemistry, which is much greener and safer than traditional C1 sources such as CO and phosgene.[7-9] The chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has attracted much attention among the world, because it not only can reduce atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration but also can produce value-added products (Figure 1). For example, the hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can yield liquid or gaseous fuels, which can mitigate the risk of fossil energy insufficiency.[10-14] However, owing to the high bond energy of the \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) and stable electronic structure of \(\pi_{3}^{4}\) in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion typically requires noble-metal catalysts and/or harsh reaction conditions, severely limiting its wide scale application in the industry.[15-18] Hence, the construction of noble-metal-free catalysts for the chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with high efficiency and recyclability is desirable, but considerably challenging.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of new catalysts that constructed from metal ions/clusters and organic ligands through coordination bonds, have attracted much attention in the field of catalysis.[19-41] Many important researches focused on hydrogenation,[42-45] reduction,[46-51] cycloaddition,[52-56] carboxylation,[57-59] and coupling reactions[60-63] have been reported in recent years. MOFs have become promising catalysts owing to the following reasons: 1) As a porous material, the pore sizes of MOFs can be tuned by regulating the sizes of organic ligands or guest template agents, thereby controlling the transfer rates of substrates and products and improving the reaction selectivity to different substrates.[64-66] 2) As heterogeneous catalysts, MOFs can be easily isolated from the reaction mixture and used in the next cycle.[67-69] 3) The catalytic sites in MOFs are highly dispersed and accessible, which facilitates the full utilization of the catalysts.[70-72] 4) The metal nodes in MOFs can be replaced with another metal, and functional groups can be grafted onto the organic ligands, so that, the activities of MOFs can be judiciously regulated for different reactions.[73-76, 5) Multiple active sites can be easily assembled into a single MOF catalyst, which may produce a synergistic effect, thus improving the catalytic efficiency.[77-79] Owing to these advantages, many research groups have focused on the thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into valuable products, and over 120 articles have been annually published
on this topic in recent years. In particular, the utilization of noble-metal-free MOF catalysts in the thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has become an important part and attracted many researchers to join in this topic, because these catalysts can reduce the industrial production costs (Scheme 1).[80,81] Researchers have provided many useful methods for improving the performance of noble-metal-free MOFs and monitoring the change of MOF structures in catalytic reaction are presented, which have a great promoting effect on the research of catalysts. In fact, although many significant reports have been published on the application of noble-metal-free MOF catalysts, the achievement of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion with high efficiency and stability still remains a challenge because of the chemical inertness and thermodynamic stability of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Hence, it is desirable to summarize the recent advances in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion using noble-metal-free MOF catalysts, and analyze the strategies for improving the performance of MOF catalysts.
This review focuses on different \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion reactions that catalyzed by noble-metal-free MOF catalysts, and is divided into four sections. The effect of different MOF structures on the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reaction performance is analyzed, followed by a discussion of the advantages and disadvantages of noble-metal-free MOF catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. Characterization methods for MOF catalysts as well as the mechanisms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion reactions are also summarized. In addition, we provide our opinions on challenges to be addressed and potential future research directions. Overall, we believe that this review would be helpful to researchers interested in this field and would motivate more researchers to explore the field of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion.
