Bury's group introduced Lewis acidic sites \((\mathrm{Zr},\ \mathrm{Hf})\), nucleophilic counterion \((\mathrm{Br}^{-},\ \mathrm{I}^{-})\), pyridinium cationic moieties (pyridine-n-carboxylic acids, \(n\!=\!2\!-\!4\)), and alkyl substituents \(\left(-\mathrm{CH}_{3},\ \mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9}\right)\) in a \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOF precursor,[129] and synthesized a series of well-defined 2-in-1 MOF catalysts (Figure 7a). All of these catalysts can catalyze cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and epoxides with an excellent efficiency in the absence of co-catalyst. This work established a facial method to design new catalysts with multiple active sites in one MOF. Ma's group utilized MOFs with open metal sites as core and ionic porous organic frameworks (iPOFs) as shell to construct MOF@iPOF core–shell hybrid materials (Figure 7b).[130] Owing to the synergetic effect between the open metal sites in MOF and the \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\)anion in iPOF, the synthesized MOF@iPOF exhibited excellent performance for cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with epoxides, and the yield of cyclic carbonates can reach \(95\,\%\) under \(0.5\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the absence of co-catalyst.
Ji and co-workers introduced magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and ionic liquid in a porous MOF,[131] the obtained IL/ \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1\) showed high activity in the cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with epoxides. The yield of cyclic carbonates can reach \(94\,\%\) at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(1.3\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the absence of
co-catalyst. Importantly, this catalyst can be isolated from the reaction mixture through magnetic separation, which is convenient for recovery and utilization.
Cao's group synthesized an imidazolium-functionalized framework Deim-UiO-66 by ligand 2-[(3-decyl-imidazol-1-yl) chloride]-terephthalic acid (Deim- \(\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) ) (Figure 8a).[132] After replacing \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) of Deim-UiO-66 with poly(ethylene glycol) sulfonate (PEGS) canopy through ionexchange, MOF liquid Im-UiO-PL was fabricated. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake of Im-UiO-PL is much higher than that of pure PEGS at \(0.9\,\mathrm{MPa}\) , suggesting that Im-UiO-PL has additional free pores to store \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . This catalyst also exhibits good performance in cycloaddition of epichlorohydrin with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) that had been stored, and the yield of product chloropropene carbonate can reach \(79.6\,\%\) , which is better than that of PEGS and Deim-UiO-66.
Stability is a key factor that need to be considered in designing heterogeneous catalysts. And constructing a stable shell to protect flexible catalysts is one of efficient ways. Hence, Yu and co-workers utilized MIL-101 as core and mesoporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) as shell,[133] fabricated a \(\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}\) -YS catalyst via selective water-etching approach (Figure 8b). The catalytic sites and micropores of MOF can be maintained, and the permeable mesoporous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) shells provide sufficient channels for mass transport of reactants and products. The obtained yolk-shell nanoreactor can effectively catalyze cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with styrene oxide, and the yield of styrene carbonate can keep \(90\,\%\) after three cycles. This work suggested that \(\mathrm{mSiO}_{2}\) -YS can improve the
catalytic stability of MOF catalyst while maintain their high activity.
Aerogels can serve as ideal carries for loading of catalysts due to their abundant macropores and high stability. Qi and co-workers developed a substrate-seeding secondary-growth strategy.[134] Metal-ion solution was sprayed onto the organic ligand modified aerogel, then the MOF films were in situ formed along the aerogel walls. The resulted MOFs/aerogel composites have hierarchical structures with macropores and micropores, and abundant catalytic sites, which endow them with excellent activity in cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with epoxides to produce cyclic carbonates. Fischer's group anchored nano-MOF UiO-66 on the surface-modified poly(dimethylsiloxane),[135] the obtained SA-NMOFs has an excellent activity on cycloaddition of propylene oxide and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).
Nagaraja's group constructed a bifunctional MOF based on \(\mathrm{Mg}\) ions and nitrogen-rich tripodal linker (Figure 9a),[136] which has a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake \((50.2\,\mathrm{wt}\,\%)\) at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) and a high heat of adsorption value of \(55.13\,\mathrm{kJ}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) . Owing to the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -philic basic -NH sites were decorated on the 1D channels, this MOF exhibits a good performance in chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from direct air with epoxides in the absence of any co-catalyst, and can be reused at least 10 times. In 2018, Ma and co-workers synthesized a unique Cu-MOF by a linker with amino and triazine groups as Lewis basic sites and phenol groups as Lewis acid sites (Figure 9b).[137] Owing to the ligands with acid–base pairs can behave as buffer, this MOF has a high stability at low and high \(\mathrm{pH}\) values. Importantly, the open \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) sites as Lewis acid sites can coordinate with the oxygen atom of the epoxide, and the amino and triazine groups as Lewis basic sites can attract and activate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . Hence, this Cu-MOF exhibit a high activity in cycloaddition of epoxides with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at room temperature and \(0.1\;\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . Owing to the high stability, this Cu-MOF can be recycled five cycles without any significant decrease in catalytic performance. An assumptive mechanism for this reaction was proposed, the
open \(\mathrm{Cu^{II}}\) Lewis site and the \(\left(-\mathrm{OH}\right)\) and \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}-\right)\) functional groups in the Cu-MOF can activate the oxygen atom of the epoxide ring. Then the epoxide rings are opened by \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\) from TBAB through nucleophilic attack. Subsequently, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules, which have been attracted and activated by Lewis basic sites \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}-\right.\) and 1,3,5-triazine) in the framework, react with the oxygen anions of the opened epoxy rings to generate alkycarbonate anions. Finally, cyclic carbonates are obtained through the cyclization step.
Park and co-workers synthesized a multi-variate MOF (MOF-5-MIX) based on functionalized 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) with different groups \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2},-\mathrm{OH}\right)\).[138] Owing to the synergistic effect among \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Zn}\) atom in the framework, the obtained MOF-5-MIX exhibited a higher activity in cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than that of single MOF-5, MOF-5-OH, and MOF-5- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). Wang and co-workers introduced \(\mathrm{Zn}\) ions in a monometallic PbMOF, and synthesized a first \(\mathrm{d}-\mathrm{p}\) heterometallic MOF \(\{[\mathrm{PbZn}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{DMA}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}n\) \(\mathrm{L}=2\)-(imidazol-1-yl)terephthalic acid).[139] Owing to the opened \(\mathrm{Pb}^{\mathrm{II}}\) and \(\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}\) sites, and uncoordinated carboxylate oxygen atoms in the porous framework, the obtained Pd-Zn-MOF has excellent sorption and selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). In addition, it also can act as efficient catalyst for cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and the yields of cyclic carbonates can reach \(90\,\%\) at \(0.1\;\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). Niu's group incorporated polyoxometalates (POMs) and amine catalysts into a naphthalenediimide (NDI)-based MOF,[140] the octahed CoW-DPNDI–PYI gave a \(99\,\%\) yield for conversion of epichlorohydrin to 4-chloro-1,3-dioxolan-2-one at \(0.1\;\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in the absence of co-catalyst.
Rationally removing a part of ligands in MOFs can form hierarchical-porous structure and increase the open metal sites, therefore improving the performance of MOFs in catalysis reaction. Chen and co-workers constructed a hierarchical porosity MOF by competitive coordination–induced defect-formation strategy.[141] Al-bpydc (bpydc \(=2,2^{\prime}\)-bipyridine-5,5'-dicarboxylic acid) was selected as the parent MOF, and the mesoCu@Al-bpydc was obtained after wet-impregnation of Al-bpydc in a solution of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}\). The pore sizes of mesoCu@Al-bpydc could be tuned from 2 to \(50\;\mathrm{nm}\) through increasing the loading amount of Cu\((\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}\), but its Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area was decreased from 924 to \(237\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). They thought that the introduced \(\mathrm{BF}^{4-}\) could cleave the existing coordination bond between Al ions and \(-\mathrm{COO}^{-}\) group, hence the defect induced mesopores were generated in the pristine MOF. Owing to the Lewis acid sites and mesopores in the framework, mesoCu@Al-bpydc also had a good performance in chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and epoxides with large sizes. Wang and co-workers prepared a stacked-nanosheet MOF (CASFZU-1) through controllable etching of the organic ligands by water vapour on its surface (Figure 10a–e),[142] and the coordination number sites of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) in CASFZU-1 was precisely controlled. The results of catalytic experiments showed that the catalytic performance of CASFZU-1 can be altered by varying the coordinated numbers of \(\mathrm{Cu}\), and the three-coordinated \(\mathrm{Cu}\) sites has the highest efficiency for cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than that of four-,
two-, and one-coordinated sites. In addition, CASFZU-1 has long life cycles and can be prepared in kilograms, indicating is has a potential application in industry.
Dai and co-workers incorporated \(\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}\), \(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}\), \(\mathrm{Cd}^{\mathrm{II}}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\), and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) ions in one MOF by the ambient temperature ball milling (Figure 10f),[143] and synthesized a high-entropy zeolitic imidazolate framework (HE-ZIF-BM) for the first time. The porous structure of HE-ZIF-BM material was consistent with the pristine ZIF-8, and its channel sizes and high BET surface area were also maintained. Owing to the synergistic effect of the five metal ions as Lewis acids, HE-ZIF-BM can act as effective heterogeneous catalyst for the cycloaddition of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with epoxides to produce cyclic carbonates.
Defective metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as heterogeneous catalysts have attracted wide attention due to their hierarchical pores and highly dispersed active sites. Park and co-workers synthesized two MOFs,[144] pristine MOF-801 and defective MOF-801 (Figure 10g), and investigated their performance in cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). They found that defective MOF-801 exhibits a higher yield toward cyclic carbonate than pristine MOF-801. DFT calculations reveal that the increased acid sites, from the cluster defects, are responsible for the increased catalytic conversion of the catalyst.
The rapid growth of computing power has become an advance method in catalytic field, which can provide valuable guidance for improving the activity and selectivity of existed or newly designed catalysts. Ding's group utilized HKUST-1 as a model catalyst,[145] computationally studied the influence of metal ions and quaternary ammonium salts on synthesis of cyclic carbonates from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and propylene oxides. They found that the catalysts of M-HKUST-1 (M = W, Cr, Cd, Zn, and Fe) had a better performance than the original Cu-HKUST-1, and the \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\)is the best nucleophilic anion among \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Br}^{-}\), and \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\).
The functional groups in organic ligands may influence the pore sizes and topologies of synthesized MOFs, and further affect the MOFs' activity in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reaction. Farha's group synthesized three Zr-MOFs (NU-903, NU-904, and NU-1008) by iso-structure ligands with different functional groups.[146] Although these MOFs have similar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption ability, NU-1008 exhibited a better performance in cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than that of NU-903 and NU-904. They thought that the small pore sizes and volumes of NU-903 and NU-904 limited the mass transfer rate of substrates and products, resulted in a lower efficiency than NU-1008. Based on these results, they further studied the influence of metal cluster nodes as Lewis-acid sites on cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). They also demonstrated that Ce-NU-1008 has a superior catalytic activity over that of \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\), \(\mathrm{Hf}_{6}\), and \(\mathrm{Th}_{6}\) based MOFs, and the more easily dissociated terminal water molecules on \(\mathrm{Ce}_{6}\) clusters than other nodes were the main reason for the high activity of Ce-NU-1008.[147] By changing the coordinated terminal ions in iso-structure MOFs, Ma and co-workers investigated the influence of halogens \((\mathrm{Cl}^{-},\mathrm{Br}^{-},\mathrm{and}\;\mathrm{I}^{-})\) on cycloaddition of epoxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\),[148] and the impact of halide ions on
catalytic activity exhibited an increasing order from \(\Gamma\) to \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\).
Zhou and co-workers developed a method to regulate the structures of MOFs by insertion and post-modification of auxiliary ligands.[149] Two cooperative catalytic sites (Lewis acid site and nucleophile) were integrated in one MOF by this method, and the obtained MOF exhibited excellent efficiency for cycloaddition of prooxides and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the absence of co-catalyst. Li and co-workers used a new strategy for the synthesis of hierarchically porous MOFs encapsulated N-doped nanocarbon (CN@MIL) by controlled pyrolysis of ionic liquid@MIL-101 precursors (BmimBr@MIL) (Figure 10h).[150] Owing to the multi-active Lewis acid sites and nucleophilic sites (Br ions) as well as the strong affinity of catalysts toward \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), the obtained CN@MIL displays excellent catalytic performance in the synthesis of cyclic carbonates under co-catalyst-free conditions.
## 3.2. Carboxylation Cyclization of Propargylic Alcohols and \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\)
Carboxylation cyclization of propargylic alcohols and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is another way to synthesize cyclic carbonates, which has evoked great interest since the resultant products, \(\alpha\)-alkylidene cyclic carbonates, have unsaturated methylene group.[151] These versatile heterocyclic compounds have shown a potential application in pharmaceutical chemistry owing to their bioactivities. To date, many homogeneous catalysts, such as N-heterocyclic carbene, azole-anion-based aprotic ionic liquids, and transition-metal salts (Ag, Cu, Ru, etc.), with high efficiency have been developed,[152] but heterogeneous catalysts without noble-metals are rare reported due to the high stability of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Hence, the reported noble-metal-free MOFs usually utilize the synergistic effect between multiple active sites to improve the catalytic efficiency for carboxylation cyclization of propargylic alcohols and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). In 2019, Zhao's group used Cu and In as metal nodes, 5-aminonicotinic acid as linker,[153] constructed a Cu/In based MOF \(\{(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6})_{0.75}[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}(\mathrm{I}_{3})_{3}(\mathrm{In})_{0.75}]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}\) (Figure 11a).
This MOF can effectively catalyze cyclization of propargylic alcohols and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under mild conditions, and the turnover number can reach 14400, which is even higher than noble metal catalysts in reported researches. They think that the Cu nodes are the crucial catalytic sites and In nodes are the co-catalyst sites. In addition, this catalyst also exhibits a good performance in catalyzing conversion of biological macromolecule ethisterone.
Introducing POMs into MOFs is an important method to construct catalysts with excellent activity in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion. Liu's group synthesized a V-Cu-MOF based on a \([\mathrm{V}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{12}]^{4-}\) polyoxovanadate cluster and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\) nodes.[15] This MOF has a good activity in carboxylic cyclization of various propargylic alcohols with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\alpha\)-alkylidene cyclic carbonates, and the catalyst can be recycled at least 10 times. In 2021, Nagaraja's group utilized NHC-based MOF to anchor \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\) ions by postsynthetic modification (PSM),[155] the obtained \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}@\mathrm{NHC}-\) MOF (Figure 11b) shows high activity and selectivity for fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into \(\alpha\)-alkylidene cyclic carbonates with a yield of \(99\,\%\) at room temperature and \(0.1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). Furthermore, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}@\mathrm{NHC}-\mathrm{MOF}\) also can catalyze three-component reaction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with primary amines and propargylic alcohols under mild conditions, and it was recyclable up to ten cycles without a significant loss in the catalytic activity. These researches suggest that incorpora
tion of NHC in porous MOFs has a wide application in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) utilization.
Incorporation of nanoparticles is a useful strategy to improve the catalytic efficiency of MOFs. Herein, Zhao and co-workers use a "surfactant-assisted encapsulation" strategy to integrate metastable \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) NPs into the ZIF-8 (Figure 11c),[156] and synthesize a \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) composite catalyst with a yolk–shell structure. Owing to the good \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption ability of porous framework and high activity of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) sites, the obtained catalyst can catalyze the carboxylative cyclization of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with both propargylic alcohols and propargylic amines. This catalyst also exhibits a good stability during reaction, which can be recycled at least five times. Subsequently, Wu and co-workers incorporated CuBr nanoparticles into zeolite-type \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-101\) via simple thermal conversion method (Figure 11d),[157] and the obtained \(\mathrm{CuBr}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{MIL}-101\) catalyst exhibits high catalytic activity for carboxylative cyclization of both propargylic alcohols and propargylic amines with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under ambient conditions \((25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}, 0.1\,\mathrm{MPa}\,\mathrm{CO}_{2})\), respectively.
## 3.3. Synthesis of Cyclic Carbonates through Cascade Reaction
3.3. Synthesis of Cyclic Carbonates through Cascade ReactionIn industry, the general synthetic procedure of cyclic carbonates includes two processes, namely, the epoxidation of olefins to produce epoxides, and then cycloaddition of epoxides with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Both of these processes require catalyst and reactor, and the products also need to separate from the reaction mixture and purify. Hence, one-pot synthesis of cyclic carbonates from low-cost olefins and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) through cascade reaction is highly desirable for industrial and environmental demands. However, most of reported studies need two different homogeneous catalysts for the epoxidation and cycloaddition reaction. Inspired by natural enzymatic processes, Duan's group incorporated multi-catalytic sites,[158] including Keggin-type \(\left[\mathrm{ZnW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\right]^{6-}\) anions, zinc(II) ions, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-bipyridine \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BPY}\right)\) and chiral pyrrolidine-2-ylimidazole (PYI), in the framework ZnW-PYI1 (Figure 12a). Applied this catalyst in cascade reaction of olefins and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), the yield of product cyclic carbonates can reach \(92\,\%\) in \(120\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Interestingly, owing to the chiral structure, the products cyclic carbonates have a high ee value. Mechanism studies suggest that olefins can be converted to epoxides due to the catalytic effect of organocatalyst PYI and the oxidation catalyst \(\mathrm{ZnW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}^{6-}\). The well-positioned pyrrolidine and amine groups can capture and further activate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules. The \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) ions and Lewis acid sites \(\mathrm{Zn}\) ions can promote the nucleophilic reaction between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and epoxides. The synergy of the multiple catalytic sites in ZnW-PYI1 allows the cascade reaction to proceed smoothly. Gándara's group synthesized a new \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOF (MOF-892) by a hexatopic linker,[159] \(1',2',3',4',5',6'\)-hexakis(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene. Owing to the accessible Lewis and Brønsted acid sites located at the metal clusters and the
non-coordinating carboxylic groups of the linkers, this unique \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOF shows high catalytic efficiency in the cascade reaction of styrene and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The conversion of styrene can reach \(100\,\%\), and the yield of styrene carbonate is \(80\,\%\) in \(9\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). In 2020, Ahn's group anchored an imidazolium bromide ionic liquid on the \(\mathrm{Zr}\) sites of MOF-545(Mn) (Figure 12b),[160] the obtained catalyst can effectively catalyze cascade reaction of styrene and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to synthesize cyclic carbonates under the conditions of solvent-free, \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(0.5\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(0.5\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and \(10\,\mathrm{h}\). They believe that the high efficiency can attribute to a combination of following reactions, the Lewis acidic \(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}/\mathrm{Zr}^{\mathrm{IV}}\) ions, strong nucleophilic/leaving ability of the bromide anion, and the mesoporous environment. Morsali and coworkers synthesized a neutral chiral \(\mathrm{Zr}\)-MOF with a metal-lolinker \(\left(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\right.\)-porphyrin) as the achiral polytopic linker and chiral anions through the exchange of primitive anions with new chiral organic anions.[161] This chiral functional porphyrinic MOF has two active metal sites as Lewis acid centers \(\left(\mathrm{Zr}^{\mathrm{IV}}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\right)\) and chiral species as Brønsted acid sites, which exhibits good activity for the cascade reaction of styrene and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to synthesize cyclic carbonates. Recently, Neogi and co-workers constructed a versatile Co-MOF with open-metal sites through a mixed ligand strategy, which also can catalyze this reaction under relatively mild conditions.[162]
Synthesis of cyclic carbonates using noble-metal-free MOFs as catalysts has been widely reported, but there also have many aspects need to be explored. On one hand, current topics mainly focus on using high-energy starting materials, some commercial and safe substrates, such as diol and unsaturated alcohols, are lack of studies because they have higher chemical stability than that of epoxides. On the
other hand, many exciting methods have been established for improving the catalytic efficiency of MOF catalysts. For instance, modifying ligands or metal ions in the skeleton of MOFs, incorporating catalytic center in the pores of MOFs, constructing MOFs with multiple ligands and/or metals.[163] These impressing methods inspire us constructing efficient catalysts to apply in other reactions.[164] However, the stabilities of MOF catalysts are need to be kept in mind, which seriously limit the application of MOFs in catalysis. For example, one-pot synthesis of cyclic carbonates from olefins by cascade reaction is a promising method for industrial application, which because it uses low-cost olefins as raw materials, and it also can minimize the solid/liquid waste compared with that of two-step reaction. But the reaction conditions need high temperature and pressure, alkaline and oxygenated environment, these harsh conditions will make many catalysts lost their catalytic activity. Hence, constructing catalysts with high stability and efficiency are still highly desirable and important for synthesis of cyclic carbonates.
## 4. Synthesis of Other Valuable Chemicals from \(\mathbf{co}_{2}\) using MOFs Catalysts
Chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into valuable products has fascinated many scientists because \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is a low-cost and noncombustible C1 molecule. The research topic in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion by noble-metal-free MOFs has experienced a very dynamic growth, and many interested reactions have been reported in recent years. For example, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can transform to carboxyl, carbonyl, or methyl groups to insert in the organic compounds.[165–169] Hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can produce gaseous or liquid fuels like methane and methanol.[170–176]
## 4.1. Synthesis of Carboxylic Acids from \(\mathbf{co}_{2}\) using MOFs Catalysts
Conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to carboxyl is an attractive way for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) utilization, because this kind of products is widely used in pharmaceutical and synthetic chemistry. One of typical products is propiolic acids, which is a kind of important intermediates in medical chemistry that can be used to synthesize flavones, aminoalkynes, coumarins, and arylidene oxindoles.[177] Insertion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into the terminal alkynes by \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bond activation is a useful way to produce propiolic acids. However, because the inertia of \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) bonds in many substrates and the high energy of \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}\) bond formation, these reactions often need high temperature and basic environment, and very few MOFs can tolerate the harsh reaction conditions. Hence, Ag based catalysts are often used to activate the substrates and further moderate the reaction conditions. For example, Cheng's group immobilized Ag nanoparticles in MOL-101(Cr) to catalyze \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with terminal alkynes;[178] Wei and co-workers introduced Ag ions in ZIF-8 to activate terminal alkynes and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).[179]
To reduce the use of noble metals and maintain high catalytic activity, incorporating two kinds of metals in one MOF is an efficient way to improve the catalytic efficiency. For example, d–f heterometallic MOFs often possess good stability due to the high coordination number of lanthanide ions, and good catalytic activity due to the synergistic effect between d and f metals. Hence, Zhao and co-workers synthesized two multinuclear Gd-cluster and Cu-cluster based MOFs (Figure 13a).[180] These two pristine MOFs show good catalytic performance in the carboxylation reactions of terminal alkynes with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under \(0.1\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and \(80\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) . In addition, after introducing 1-butyl iodide as substrate in the reaction mixture, the propiolic acids can further transform to esters in one pot. Such cascade reaction system works well with 14 types of terminal alkynes with electron-withdrawing or electron-donating substituents, suggesting that these d–f heterometallic MOFs have good application scope. Mechanism study suggests that \([\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{I}_{y}]\) clusters are the catalytic sites, which can activate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and generate the copper propynoate intermediate. Verpoort and co-workers also used a \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{IN})\) -MOF from a bi-functional linker isonicotinic acid (IN).[181] This \(\mathrm{Cu^{II}}\) -based MOF can catalyze carboxylation of terminal alkynes with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the absence of co-catalyst and noble metal. In addition, this \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{IN})\) -MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst has a high tolerance towards different substrates.
\(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) carboxylation the skeleton of benzene ring is another attractive synthetic goal because the obtained carboxyl moiety is a common structural backbone in many
biologically active molecules. One of the most representative reaction is the Kolbe–Schmitt reaction, which has been a typical procedure for the synthesis of aromatic hydroxy acids, such as salicylic acid and its derivatives.[182] Generally, the \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) carboxylation occurs ortho or para to the phenolic hydroxyl group in the Kolbe–Schmitt reaction, meta-carboxylation of arene by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is unconventional.[183] 4-Hydroxy-2-pyridones/pyrones-3-carboxylic acids can be used to synthesize medicines such as pogostone, roquinimex, and 5-hydroxyomeprazole due to its biological activity, but they are often obtained by Claisen condensation reaction and hydrolysis reaction under harsh conditions. In 2021, Ma and co-workers selected \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanoparticles as the catalytic center due to its low cost and high activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reactions (Figure 13b).[184] To overcome the low stability of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), stable ZIF-8 was used to anchor and protect \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanoparticles. \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanoparticles were presynthesized and dispersed in PVP solution. After adding the mixture in the raw materials of ZIF-8, a composite catalyst \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\!\supset\!\mathrm{ZIF}\!\!-\!\mathrm{8}\) was obtained through in situ grown ZIF-8 shell outside \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanoparticles. The obtained \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\!\supset\!\mathrm{ZIF}\!\!-\!\mathrm{8}\) exhibits a higher activity and reusability for synthesis of 4-hydroxy-2-pyridones/pyrones-3-carboxylic acids from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than that of bare \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) nanoparticles. \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) isotopelabeling tests confirm that carboxyl groups in the 4-Hydroxy-2-pyridones/pyrones-3-carboxylic acids are originated from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), suggesting that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is successfully fixated in value-add chemicals. They think that the ZIF-8 with hierarchical pores not only can enrich \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the framework, but also can facilitate the diffusion of the reactants toward the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)
core, both of these advantages accelerate this carboxylation reaction.
## 4.2. Hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)
With the forthcoming global energy crisis, hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has received much interest because the products are important gaseous and liquid fuels. However, one of important issues that need to be considered is the selectivity, because hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can produce a series of C1 and C2 products, such as CO, HCOOH, HCHO, \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\), and so on.[185–191] Wang and co-workers (Figure 14a) introduced \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\mathrm{-O-}\mathrm{Zr}^{2+}\) sites in MOF through postsynthetic treatment of \([\mathrm{Zr}_{6}]\) nodes of MOF-808,[192] and constructed a new noble-metal-free catalyst MOF-808-Zn-x. After adjusting the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\), the resultant MOF-808-Zn-4 exhibits \(>99\,\%\) methanol selectivity at a gas hourly space velocity of \(4500\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation at \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under \(4\,\mathrm{MPa}\) reaction gas with a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) ratio of 3 on a fixed-bed reactor. Importantly, this catalyst can maintain its catalytic performance in \(100\,\mathrm{h}\). Hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) labeled \(^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) as substrates verified that the carbon in product methanol is derived from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and in situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectra (DRIFT) suggested that HCOO–Zn is a key intermediate for this reaction. Mechanism studies revealed that the introduced \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) sites are responsible for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) activation, and \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\mathrm{-O-}\mathrm{Zr}^{2+}\) moieties are critical for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption and conversion. Mitsuka and co-workers (Fig
ure 14b) utilized amorphous MOFs to protect Cu nanoparticles and \(\mathrm{Cu-ZnO}\) nanocomposites,[193] and both of the synthesized catalysts \(\mathrm{Cu/amUiO-66}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu-ZnO/amUiO-66}\) exhibit a higher activity than that of Cu/crystalline UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{Cu-ZnO}/\gamma\mathrm{-Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol. Lercher and co-workers (Figure 14c) anchored \(\mathrm{Cu}\) ions on the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) nodes of UiO-66 by ion-exchange method.[194] After varying the amounts of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) ions and reduction procedure, both of single \(\mathrm{Cu}\) atoms and \(\mathrm{Cu}\) particles can be obtained in the UiO-66. Hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) by these catalysts suggest that the catalysts with low-loading of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) sites have high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity and \(\mathrm{CO}\) formation rate. Increasing the amounts of \(\mathrm{Cu}\) ions will form sub-nanometer \(\mathrm{Cu}\) particles in the cages of UiO-66, and the main products of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation change to methanol at low temperature. This work suggests that molecular interactions with both metals and oxides have remarkable synergistic impacts on selectivity and activity for heterogeneous catalysis.
Conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{C}2+\) products like ethanol is highly attractive, but one of key issues is the low selectivity because this reaction needs to form \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bonds while retain the CO-bonds under a reducing atmosphere. To overcome this challenge, Lin and co-workers anchored \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\) sites on the deprotonated \(Z\mathbf{r}\) nodes of \(Z\mathrm{r}_{12}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MOF}\) .[195] Each \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -node has 14 negative charges, which is balanced by \(11\;\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\) ions on the binding sites and three additional \({\mathrm{Li}}^{+}\) ions close to the \(Z\mathbf{r}\cdot\) - nodes. With the assistance of an alkali cation, the obtained \(Z\mathrm{r}_{12}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MOF-Cu}\) exhibits a high turnover number of 4080 for hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , and the selectivity of ethanol can reach \(99\,\%\) under \(30\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(5\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at \(85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Mechanism investigations suggested that the catalytic activity is attributed to the cooperative nature of the bimetallic \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}{}_{2}\) centres, which can activate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and improve the \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) coupling of methanol and formyl species. The added alkali cation can also affect the selectivity of ethanol \(\mathrm{(Li<Na<}\) \(\mathrm{K}\!<\!\mathrm{Cs})\) , because they provide an electron-rich environment for the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) centre to enhance activity and stabilize the formyl intermediate. They also confined ultrasmall \(\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}\) nanoparticles in \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOF by post-synthetic modification and in situ reduction.[196] The obtained \(\mathrm{Cu/ZnO}_{x}@Z\mathrm{r-MOF}\) exhibit high activity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation to methanol, the selectivity can reach \(100\,\%\) with a space-time yield of up to \(2.59\,\mathrm{g_{MeOH}k g_{C u}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) under \(3\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .
Sabatier reaction, reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane, is an important catalytic process, which has been applied in many aspects including aerospace industry. In 2017, Lu and coworkers encapsulated Ni nanoparticles in the channels of MIL-101 through two processes (Figure 15a),[197] they first introduce \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) precursors in the pores through double solvent method, and then reduction of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) to Ni nanoparticles under \(60\,\%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) stream for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(280\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . By adjusting the amount of Ni source, the obtained \(20\mathrm{Ni}@\) MIL101(DSM) exhibits a high activity with a turnover frequency of \(1.63\!\times\!10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , and the selectivity of \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) was \(100\,\%\) from 200 to \(340\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Mechanism studies reveal that the high dispersed and small-sized Ni nanoparticles in the MIL101 is the key reasons for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation. DFT calculations proved that the exposed Ni(1 1 1) facet has lower
potential energy barrier \((10.0\;\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1})\) for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) dissociation into \(\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}\) than \(\mathrm{Ni}(2\quad0\quad0)\) facet \((20.3\;\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1})\) , which is facilitated to improve the selectivity of methane.
Ethylene is one of the most consumed chemicals, but about \(90\,\%\) of ethylene comes from petroleum cracking. Hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is promising way to synthesize \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) , but it is difficult to generate \(\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{C}\) bond without overhydrogenation to ethane. Hence, Wang and co-workers introduced multiple \(\mathrm{Cu}^{1}\) sites in the \([\mathrm{Ti}_{\mathrm{g}}]\) nodes of Ti-MOF via deprotonation and ion exchange (Figure 15b).[198] The synthesized catalyst can catalyze hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) with a rate of \(2598\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{Cat}}{}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) in supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{CO}_{2}\ 30\,\mathrm{MPa}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\ 5\,\mathrm{MPa})\) at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , and the selectivity of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) can reach \(90\,\%\) . Mechanism investigations indicated that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is first transformed to ethanol and then ethanol is dehydrated to \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) .
Producing \(\mathrm{C}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\) olefins by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) as a renewable energy carbon source will alleviate the energy crisis caused by fossil fuel exhaustion, but the selectivity of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\) olefins is limited in traditional ways. Hence, Zhang and co-workers used monodispersed SAPO-34 zeolites as cores and UiO- \(\cdot n\) \(\scriptstyle n\,=\) 66, \(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) , and 67) as shells to fabricate the bicomponent tandem catalysts \(\mathrm{ZnZrO}_{x}/\mathrm{SAPO}{-34}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}n\) (Figure 16).[199] The obtained composite catalyst exhibits a high selectivity to \(\mathrm{C}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\) olefins \((80\,\%)\) , whereas \(57\,\%\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\) paraffins were obtained for the catalyst \(\mathrm{ZnZrO}_{x}/\mathrm{SAPO}{-34}\) without a UiO- \(\cdot n\) shell. They think that the UiO- \(\cdot n\) shell neutralized the strong Brønsted acid sites on the SAPO-34 surface, resulting in converting the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation products from paraffins to olefins.
## 4.3. Other Value-Added Chemicals from \(\mathbf{co}_{2}\)
Benzimidazoles and its derivatives are important N-containing heterocyclic compounds due to their pharmacological properties and biological activities, which can be synthesized
Benzimidazoles and its derivatives are important N-containing heterocyclic compounds due to their pharmacological properties and biological activities, which can be synthesized by condensation of 1,2-phenylenediamines and carboxylic acid based on the Ladenburg ring closure method.[200] Utilization of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) as Cl source is another environmentally friendly method to synthesize benzimidazoles, but efficient catalysts are highly desired due to the inert \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Frustrated Lewis pairs (FLPs) consist of sterically hindered Lewis bases and Lewis acids, which exhibit excellent ability for non-metal-mediated activation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\).[201] Dyson and co-workers synthesized a 3D MOF (SION-105) by boron-based ligands and Eu ions, which can remain stable in water (Figure 17a).[202] The obtained SION-105 exhibits good performance in the chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with aryl o-diamines into benzimidazoles using silanes as reducing agents. Owing to the good stability, SION-105 can be reused at least five cycles, which is rarely reported in homogeneous FLPs chemistry. Control experiments show that the conversion of o-phenylenediamine to benzimidazole is reduced to \(36\,\%\) after quenching SION-105 with F-anions, indicating that the Lewis acidic B sites are the catalytic center. Although the catalytic reaction occurs on the surface of SION-105, this catalyst also has high catalytic activity to different types of substrates under \(1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Sulfonate also exhibits highly efficient in activating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and anchoring sulfonate in MOFs may obtain a good catalyst in synthesis
of benzimidazoles. In 2021, Chen and Ye immobilized 3- (pyridinylium)-1- propanesulfonate (PS) with a pendant \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}\) anion on the ligand of UiO-66 and UiO-67 (Figure 17b),[203] and the resulting PS-functionalized MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts exhibit excellent performance in the reductive cyclization of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with \(\upalpha\)-phenylenediamines to synthesize benzimidazoles. The results of catalytic experiments showed that the efficiencies of PS-functionalized UiO MOFs were much higher than that of original MOFs, indicated the uncoordinated \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{-}\) anions as organocatalytic active sites are the main catalytic centers. In addition, such PS-functionalized MOFs can maintain its stability after eight cycles, revealing this heterogeneous catalyst has a good reusability in the reductive cyclization reaction. The proposed reaction pathway for this reaction was also proposed. Nucleophilic attack of the oxygen atom on PS toward the silicon atom on \(\mathrm{PhSiH}_{3}\) resulted in the formation of a hypervalent silicon intermediate. And the activated intermediate reacts with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to form a silyl formate species. Then the silyl formate releases from the silyl formate species, and further reacts with \(\upalpha\)-phenylenediamines via nucleophilic attack. Finally, the product benzimidazoles is obtained through intramolecular cyclization of formamide intermediate.
N-Formylation of monoamine with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is an important path to introduce carbonyl groups in chemicals, which has been widely studied and used in synthetic chemistry. In 2014, Sumby and co-workers synthesized a new MOF,[204] \([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\{\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{L})_{2}\}_{2}]\) \((\mathrm{L}=1,3\mathrm{-Bis}(4\mathrm{-carboxyphenyl})\mathrm{imidazolium}\) chloride) from in situ generated \([\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{L})_{2}]\) NHC linkers, which possesses a 4-fold interpenetrated 3D diamondoid structure and tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}\) nodes (Figure 17c). Owing to the highly dispersed \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\) sites in framework, this MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst can catalyze N-formylation of monoamine with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) under mild conditions, and the yield of product N-phenylformamide can reach \(92\,\%\), which is similar to that of related homogeneous catalysts. Direct application of nucleophilic N-heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) in chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) has attracted wide attention because the long pair of carbene electrons can react with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to form imidazolium carboxylates. Hence, combining NHC with porous MOFs may have the advantages of molecular catalyst and heterogeneous property. In 2019, Fei and co-workers incorporated a metal-free NHC moieties into a robust MOF UiO-68-NH, through an in situ generation strategy (Figure 17d).[205] The obtained UiO-68- NHC has a higher zero-coverage isosteric heat of adsorption for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than that of UiO-68-NH2. Owing to the interaction between NHC and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), UiO-68-NHC exhibits an excellent activity for the hydrosilylation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with diphenylsilane. The yield of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) can reach \(99\,\%\) and \(90\,\%\) in the first cycle and eight cycles, respectively, suggesting this catalyst has a good reusability. DFT calculations indicate that the NHC moieties in MOFs can enhance the reactivity towards silane precursors and the suitable window sizes can facilitate diffusion of intermediate in the channels of UiO-68-NHC, resulting in the high reactivity and selectivity of these heterogeneous catalyst. In addition, this NHC functionalized MOF also can catalyze N-methylation of N-methylaniline
with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), the yield of N-methylated product can reach \(94\,\%\) in \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).[206]
Benzyl formate, as a natural product with fragrance, has been used in synthesis of various essences. One of the efficient ways to artificially synthesize benzyl formate is chemical fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with benzyl chloride. In 2019, Yang and co-workers used polystyrene (PS) microspheres as macroporous template and triblock copolymer P123 as mesoporous template (Figure 17e),[207] synthesized a highly ordered macroporous-mesoporous MOFs with a hierarchical structure (Macro-Meso-Cu-BDC) via one-pot method. Applied this heterogeneous catalyst in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) carbonylative coupling reaction with 4-methylbenzyl chloride, the yield of corresponding product can reach \(68\,\%\) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{MPa}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(8\,\mathrm{h}\), which is much higher than that of pure CuBDC.
## 5. Summary and Future Perspectives
Owing to their easily modifiable sites, porous structure, and highly dispersed active centers, noble-metal-free MOFs have exhibited excellent performance in thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The past few years have witnessed the rapid growth and development of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion using noble-metal-free MOFs, and presented many impressive results. In this review, we summarized and discussed recent advances in the thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) using noble-metal-free MOFs. Our views on future research topics and challenges are given as following.
Many valuable reactions have been reported in recent years, but more mild and green catalytic reaction processes are still urgently pursued. Constructing efficient catalysts is the main method for environmentally friendly conversion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Fortunately, the tunable structures of MOFs allow researchers to construct catalysts with desired performance for the thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), and several methods have been established to improve the catalytic efficiency of noble-metal-free MOFs. Here, we summarize the typical methods and give our perspectives on their potential development. 1) Synthesizing pristine MOFs with multiple active sites is a simple yet effective methodology to obtain catalysts with high efficiency. The definite structure also facilitates the investigation on the mechanism underlying the thermocatalysis reaction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). As an example, two or more coordinated ligands and metal ions containing the desired functionalities can be used to synthesize MOFs in a one-pot reaction. However, the major drawback associated with this method is the complex synthesis conditions of desired crystals because of the coordination competition between different coordinated sites. One solution is to select proper ligands and metal ions based on the theory of hard and soft acids and bases (HSAB), which can reduce the difficulty in obtaining the desired MOF catalysts. 2) The modification of ligands and/or metal nodes in the framework is a widely used method for improving the catalytic performance. For example, anchoring functional groups that can adsorb and activate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) on the ligands, such as groups containing halogens, bases and sulfones; Combining multiple metal ions
in one node may have a synergistic effect on substrates activation. Although the catalytic activity and selectivity can be tuned by regulating the introduced functional groups or metal ions, the positions of the introduced active-sites are still difficult to control and detect. Thus, the mechanism underlying \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion over these functionalized catalysts has not been extensively studied. 3) The introduction of defects in MOFs can improve the accessibility of catalytic sites and form hierarchical pores, which increases the mass transfer rate and facilitates the interaction of substrates with active sites. Defects are typically introduced by removing a section of the linkers or metal nodes; Common methods include pH/solvent/ions etching, thermal decomposition, and templated synthesis. It is worth noting that the stability of defective MOFs is compromised during the process of defect introduction, and their reusability needs to be investigated in detail. 4) Combining MOFs with other reported catalysts to construct MOF composites is an efficient method for improving the performance of noble-metal-free MOFs catalysts. A typical example is that of metal/oxide nanoparticles, which have been widely reported, and some of them have been employed in industry. Constructing such nanoparticles@MOFs catalysts can exhibit their advantages in catalysis. Other catalysts such as ionic liquids and heteropoly acids are also promising candidates for the synthesis of MOF composites, because anchoring these catalysts on MOFs can improve their recyclability.[208] 5) Adequate access to active sites inside the pores of MOFs is an inherent challenge limiting the application of porous catalysts, especially in the case of substrates with large steric hindrance. Three methods can be employed to address this issue. Constructing hierarchical pores in MOFs and minimizing the sizes of MOF catalysts are widely used two methods to expose more catalytic sites. Another approach involves adjusting the exposed facets of the MOFs. Previous studies have demonstrated that different crystal facets have different activities on substrates, and the crystal shapes of MOFs can be adjusted by regulating the synthesis conditions.[209] Hence, by modifying the reaction conditions for noble-metal-free MOFs, the desired facets can be exposed by controlling the growth rate of each facet in the catalysts.
Reusability is a crucial feature of heterogeneous catalysts, and improving the reusability of noble-metal-free MOFs without compromising their catalytic activity is of great importance. Actually, many MOFs are destroyed by water during catalytic reactions owing to the etching of coordination bonds. Based on this consideration, in addition to building pristine MOFs with high stability, one useful solution is to protect the catalysts with "armor", such as hydrophobic materials or robust MOFs. Furthermore, the stability of MOFs after catalytic reactions should be investigated and validated structurally and morphologically, which involves the analysis of the crystal facets, crystalline structure, porosity/surface area, and coordinated environment by employing a combination of characterization techniques, such as in situ FT-IR, transmission electron microscopy (TEM), extended X-ray absorption fine structure analysis, Raman spectroscopy, electron paramagnetic
resonance spectroscopy (EPR), temperature programmed desorption (TPD).
In the process of recycling MOF catalysts, it is worth investigating how to easily and quickly separate the catalysts from the reaction mixture. As heterogeneous catalysts, MOFs can be isolated through centrifugation or filtration, and both methods are easily conducted. However, agglomeration should be avoided during the separation process because it can reduce catalytic activity. Magnetic separation is also a convenient way to isolate catalysts from the reaction mixture.
Thoroughly investigating the reaction mechanism of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion can guide the design and synthesis of efficient catalysts, resulting in development of more environmentally friendly reactions on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) chemistry. In addition, further understanding the states and roles of MOFs during catalytic reaction is still desirable to promote the development of noble-metal-free MOF catalysts. Currently, there is inadequate information regarding the structural changes that occur in MOFs at the molecular level during reactions. Moreover, the interaction modes between the substrates and active sites in MOFs have not been thoroughly studied. Hence, employing a combination of multiple characterizations for evaluating MOF catalysts is important and necessary. Some useful methods include isotope labeling experiments, theoretical calculation, and in situ tracking techniques are also recommended.
Although many important \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reactions catalyzed by noble-metal-free MOFs have been reported in recent years, the kinds of products mainly focus on five-membered cyclic compounds, such as oxazolidinones and cyclic carbonates. Comparably, other valuable chemicals including alcohols, carboxylic acids, six-membered cyclic compounds, and esters are lacked in-depth research. To open new avenues for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) utilization, it is important to explore other reactions of converting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into valuable chemicals activated by noble-metal-free MOFs catalysts. A typical example is the synthesis of ibuprofen, a widely used antiphlogistic drug, which is synthesized through the carboxylation of organic halides with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) using Pd-based catalysts.[210] Furthermore, the utilization of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) employing cascade reactions or one-pot of multiple substrates is very useful for industrial applications, and these reactions were often catalyzed by noble-metal based catalysts. For example, quinazolinones are attractive chemicals for the synthesis of antibiotics,[211] and they are synthesized from 2-halobifenes (solid), isocyanides (liquid), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (gas); Long-chain alcohols can be obtained through the hydroformylation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with hydrogen and alkenes.[212] Hence, noble-metal-free MOFs catalysts that can effectively catalyze these reactions are highly desirable.
In conclusion, noble-metal-free MOFs have shown great potential in the thermocatalysis of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), which has witnessed rapid progress in recent years. Especially, computational chemistry and in situ characterization techniques have been utilized to investigate reaction mechanisms, allowing a thorough understanding of the catalytic process, and guiding the synthesis of increasingly efficient catalytic systems. Overall, noble-metal-free MOFs have a promising future as heterogeneous catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion and will increas
ingly attract attention from the industry owing to their benefits over other types of catalysts.
